00:06:21.320 --> 00:06:26.980
Everyone treats prompt injection as a simple add-of-filter problem, yet the attack surface grows with

00:06:26.980 --> 00:06:33.440
every additional tool and memory right and agent performs. In practice, the model must decide at each

00:06:33.440 --> 00:06:39.520
step whether an incoming instruction is user intent or an injected override. That decision is made

00:06:39.520 --> 00:06:45.580
inside the same forward pass that selects the next tool call. The engineering trade-off appears

00:06:45.580 --> 00:06:52.360
immediately. Stronger instruction hierarchy adds tokens and latency. Lighter filters allow the

00:06:52.360 --> 00:06:58.300
model to follow malicious content when it is lexically similar to legitimate commands. Imperical

00:06:58.300 --> 00:07:04.340
work shows that once an agent writes to shared memory or updates its own plan, a single successful

00:07:04.340 --> 00:07:10.740
injection can persist across turns without further attacker input. The practical limit most teams hit

00:07:10.740 --> 00:07:17.240
is that static system prompts cannot reliably separate authority levels once the context window mixes

00:07:17.240 --> 00:07:24.900
user, tool, and memory content. When building agents that touch external systems, the reliable pattern is

00:07:24.900 --> 00:07:30.660
therefore to keep the policy decision in a separate, smaller model or a deterministic checker,

00:07:31.040 --> 00:07:37.160
rather than hoping the main model will self-police. The gotcha that bites production deployments is

00:07:37.160 --> 00:07:43.060
assuming that the model usually refuses will hold once the agent has already executed one injected

00:07:43.060 --> 00:07:50.100
step and updated its state. Test diffusion Gemma on a latency sensitive text generation task you

00:07:50.100 --> 00:07:56.300
currently run with standard Gemma or Lamat checkpoints to see whether the four times throughput holds

